Customer Segmentation & Treatment Effect Analysis — Project Game Plan
Project Overview
This project applies unsupervised and supervised machine learning to identify customer segments and measure how a treatment variable impacts behavior across those segments. The methodology is generalizable to any A/B testing or policy change scenario where you need to identify which customer groups respond differently to an intervention.
Timeline
	•	Junior data scientist: 2–3 weeks
	•	Experienced data scientist: 10–14 days

Week 1 — Foundation & Exploration
Day 1–2: Exploratory Data Analysis
	•	Load data, inspect shape, data types, and missing values
	•	Review distributions of key variables
	•	Compare test vs. control groups at a high level to confirm a measurable signal exists
	•	Document data quality issues to address before modeling
Goal: Understand the data before committing to a modeling approach.
Day 3–4: Feature Engineering & Selection
	•	Engineer features from raw data (ratios, time-based aggregates, behavioral trends)
	•	Run a quick baseline random forest to extract feature importance scores
	•	Drop features with near-zero importance
	•	Check for multicollinearity using Variance Inflation Factor (VIF)
	•	Lock the test/control indicator as a forced feature for supervised modeling
Goal: Build a clean, meaningful feature matrix.
Day 5: Data Quality Lock
	•	Final check for missing values, leakage, and structural issues
	•	Confirm feature set is ready for clustering
Goal: Enter modeling phase with clean inputs.

Week 2 — Modeling
Day 6–7: KMeans Clustering
	•	Run KMeans without the test/control indicator to find natural customer segments
	•	Use the elbow method and silhouette score to determine optimal number of clusters
	•	Generate cluster profiles to characterize each segment
Goal: Identify natural customer groupings independent of treatment exposure.
Day 8: Decision Tree Baseline
	•	Train a decision tree to predict cluster assignment
	•	Include the test/control indicator as a feature
	•	Constrain max depth (typically 5–6) to prevent overfitting
	•	Document decision paths for interpretability
Goal: Establish an interpretable baseline model.
Day 9: Random Forest with Cross-Validation
	•	Train a random forest with 100 trees
	•	Apply 5-fold cross-validation to validate model stability
	•	Pull feature importance and compare to decision tree results
Goal: Improve accuracy and validate model generalizability.
Day 10: XGBoost with Hyperparameter Tuning
	•	Use grid search or randomized search to tune learning rate, max depth, and number of estimators
	•	Apply 5-fold cross-validation
	•	Compare results to random forest and decision tree
Goal: Push accuracy to the highest level and confirm model convergence.

Week 3 — Analysis & Reporting
Day 11: Evaluation & Significance Testing
	•	Compare key outcome metrics between test and control groups within each cluster
	•	Apply appropriate statistical tests (chi-square, t-tests, or McNemar’s test)
	•	Identify clusters with statistically significant treatment effects
Goal: Connect model output to actionable insights.
Day 12: Interpretation & Visualization
	•	Synthesize findings across all three models
	•	Identify which segments benefit from the treatment and which don’t
	•	Build visualizations (cluster profiles, treatment impact charts, feature importance plots)
Goal: Translate model output into a clear narrative.
Day 13–14: Documentation & Handoff
	•	Write a README covering business problem, methodology, findings, recommendations
	•	Clean and comment code, organize by phase
	•	Create a final summary deck for stakeholders
	•	Provide clear next steps and limitations
Goal: Deliver a reproducible, defensible final product.

Evaluation Metrics
Use multiple metrics together — no single metric tells the full story.
	•	Accuracy — overall correctness; misleading on imbalanced data
	•	ROC-AUC — model’s ability to separate classes (target above 0.8)
	•	Precision — when the model predicts positive, how often it’s right
	•	Recall — of all actual positives, how many the model catches
	•	F1 Score — harmonic mean of precision and recall
Cross-Validation
Apply 5-fold cross-validation to all supervised models. Cross-validation:
	•	Splits data into 5 train/test combinations
	•	Trains and tests on each split
	•	Averages results for a reliable performance estimate
	•	Detects overfitting and instability
Class Imbalance
If a binary target is severely imbalanced, apply SMOTE to the training data only. SMOTE artificially balances training data so the model learns meaningful patterns rather than defaulting to the majority class.
Hyperparameter Tuning
Use grid search or randomized search with cross-validation. Key parameters:
	•	Decision Tree: max_depth, min_samples_split
	•	Random Forest: n_estimators, max_depth, max_features
	•	XGBoost: learning_rate, max_depth, n_estimators, subsample

Throughout the Project
	•	Check in after each major phase (EDA, feature engineering, clustering, each model)
	•	Challenge assumptions before presenting results
	•	Document decisions and trade-offs as you go
	•	Keep a retrospective list of what worked and what didn’t
Final Deliverables
	•	Clean, reproducible code repository
	•	Executive summary with findings and recommendations
	•	Technical documentation of methodology
	•	Visualizations of segment profiles and treatment impact
	•	Appendix with all evaluation metrics and statistical test results

Key Principles
	1.	Start simple, build complexity progressively. Decision tree → Random Forest → XGBoost.
	2.	Interpretability vs. accuracy is a real trade-off. Use simple models to explain, complex models to validate.
	3.	KMeans is unsupervised — no target variable. Supervised models come after.
	4.	Cross-validation is mandatory for any supervised modeling claim.
	5.	Multiple metrics tell the full story — never rely on accuracy alone.
	6.	Significance testing connects models to business decisions — don’t skip it.
	7.	Convergence across models builds confidence. When decision tree, random forest, and XGBoost agree, you have a defensible answer.

